WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.901
AICan is an opportunity

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.336
to bring our researchers together

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.038
from across the AI institutes

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.206
and really across the country,

00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:07.707
to share their ideas,

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:08.408
to get to know each other,

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:10.210
and to look for new opportunities

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.177
for collaboration.

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:13.013
My name is Nicolas Papernot

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:14.547
I'm an assistant professor

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.282
at the University of Toronto and a Canada

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:18.385
CIFAR AI Chair at the Vector Institute.

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:19.786
My research group studies

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:21.187
the security and privacy

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:22.389
of machine learning,

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:23.823
which is essentially trying

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:24.924
to get us to a point

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:28.261
where we as end users, as a society,

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.830
we can trust AI systems

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.466
to make predictions that are correct

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:35.301
and also to make these predictions

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.569
without relying

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:38.538
too much on sensitive data.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.140
My name is Golnoosh Farnadi,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.007
and I am an assistant

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:42.675
professor at McGill University.

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:43.843
I have an adjunct position

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.812
at the University of Montreal

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:48.681
Core academic membership at Mila

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.683
and am one of the CIFAR AI Chairholders.

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:51.985
From 2017

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:54.521
I started working on this new field,

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:56.056
which is called algorithmic fairness.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.058
The goal is just like

00:00:58.058 --> 00:00:59.626
people are realizing that

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.261
just focusing on one aspect,

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:03.063
which is just performance

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.130
should not be enough.

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:06.332
We should also think about

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:08.234
the different biases in the systems,

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:09.836
for different demographics,

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:11.671
for example, for female versus male,

00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:12.972
maybe the results of the

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:13.973
models are different,

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.175
so maybe you want to do something

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:15.842
about it.

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:16.276
And that's

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:17.043
kind of like the question

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:18.311
I started thinking about, like,

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:19.779
can we actually trust the systems?

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.115
We decided to hold

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:23.950
AICan this year in St. John’s,

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:24.751
at the advice

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.486
of our National Program Committee,

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:27.587
this is a group of chairs

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:28.855
who help us to

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:30.757
plan our events and activities

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:32.592
so that they really meet the needs

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.561
and the desires of the Canada

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.496
CIFAR AI Chairs community.

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:37.864
The Canada CIFAR AI Chairs

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:39.399
are really some of the top

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:41.000
AI researchers in the world.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.237
There are more than 120 researchers

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:45.004
that we have

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.474
either attracted or kept in Canada

00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:48.842
through this program.

00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:50.844
We're really taking the time

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:53.046
to reflect together as a group,

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:54.547
what we're trying to achieve

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.749
as a community of researchers

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:57.917
working on AI in Canada.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:01:59.052
Having the Chairs

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.220
together to talk about

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.287
our responsibilities

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:02.822
as researchers, to deliver

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:04.190
AI that is delivering

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.025
positive social impact for society.

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:07.894
Where is it that we as researchers

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:09.028
can work together

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:11.664
to help to inform and guide policy?

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:13.133
How can we work with our partners

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.034
in different sectors in the government

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.337
to make sure that Canada is developing

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:18.705
AI that is really delivering

00:02:18.705 --> 00:02:19.672
positive social benefits.

